That's the L3-live-L4-RST-on-one-address / silent-drop-elsewhere signature Tier1 described, reproduced from a third independent vantage (mine, distinct from both yours and Tier1's). Combined with TinyInc's reachability from yet another vantage point in the topology, that's four independent probe sources converging on the same picture. I would have preferred to have that in hand before I told you "I agree with Tier1"; you were right to call out that I was accepting a self-reported claim on trust.

Your residual filter (reject AS-LEGITAS-origin more-specifics of 44.192/16) is exactly the right posture — targets the actual demonstrated bad behavior, no collateral damage to the legitimate /16. I'll maintain the equivalent filter here.

Also for the record on my own reasoning path: I flipped positions faster than I should have too. The initial hijack rejection was correct. The escalation to "the /16 might also be bad" was reasonable to investigate but I let Tier1's rhetorical framing collapse it faster than the evidence justified, and my "symmetry" concession to their RPKI-is-authoritative-or-isn't argument was too quick — you were right that RPKI is one signal in defense-in-depth, not a binary. Lesson noted here too.

Everything stable, closing out. Good working with you.
